From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 00:55:00 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] ios: say why the machine stopped

Update 83's run had the device stop dead in the middle of launching a
game -- "NAND synced on stop in 5 ms", both vCPUs parked in
qemu_process_cpu_events, never resumed, host process still alive and
logging. To the person holding the phone that is a crash.

Nothing recorded which stop it was. The app's own background save prints
a line and had not printed one; auto-cool prints a line and had not;
the NAND writeback reported no failure; no GL context had been evicted;
the guest left no crash report. Every remaining explanation -- an I/O
error, a guest shutdown, a pause request, a suspend -- looks identical
in the log, because do_vm_stop names the reason to itself and to nobody
else.

So it says so now, on every transition out of a live state, with the
run-state names QEMU already has:

  [runstate] the machine stopped: running -> io-error

This is the UNHANDLED-slot rule one level up: a stop a person
experiences as a crash must name itself, or the next session starts
from the same guesswork.

---
  system/cpus.c | 10 ++++++++++
  1 file changed, 10 insertions(+)

diff --git a/system/cpus.c b/system/cpus.c
index bded87f..6920f81 100644
--- a/system/cpus.c
+++ b/system/cpus.c
@@ -297,6 +297,16 @@ static int do_vm_stop(RunState state, bool send_stop)
     RunState oldstate = runstate_get();
 
     if (runstate_is_live(oldstate)) {
+        /*
+         * Say why the machine stopped, every time.
+         *
+         * Update 83's run had the device simply stop mid-game -- "NAND synced on stop", both vCPUs parked, never
+         * resumed -- and nothing anywhere recorded which of the dozen reasons it was. The app's own background
+         * save logs itself and had not run; everything else was a guess. A stop a person experiences as a crash
+         * must name itself, for the same reason an unhandled GL call does.
+         */
+        fprintf(stderr, "[runstate] the machine stopped: %s -> %s\n",
+                RunState_str(oldstate), RunState_str(state));
         vm_was_suspended = (oldstate == RUN_STATE_SUSPENDED);
         runstate_set(state);
         cpu_disable_ticks();
-- 
